1. properties visibility
    - there are 3 types of visibility: public (can be accessed everywhere), protected (can be accessed in parent class
    and in its inherited ones) and private (can be accessed only in the very class).
    there is also asymmetric property visibility: different scope for reading (get) and writing (set); set visibility
    cannot be more permissive than the default visibility ( public private(set) string $name )

2. magic methods
    - those are special methods that are automatically executed by php when certain actions are performed on an object.
    they are: __construct(), __destruct(), _call(), __get(), __set(), __isset(), __unset(), __invoke(), __serialize(),
    __unserialize(), __sleep(), __wakeup(), __toString(), __debugInfo()
        __construct() - executed when object is initialized
        __destruct() - vice versa
        __call() - executed when invoking inaccessible methods
        __get() - triggered when reading data from inaccessible or non-existing properties
        __set() - is run when writing data to inaccessible or non-existing properties
        __isset() - triggered by calling isset() or empty() on inaccessible or non-existing properties
        __unset() - same but with unset()
        __invoke() - executed when ure trying to call an object as a function ( $obj(5) )
        __serialize() - defines which ones of object properties will be serialized
        __unserialize() - defines how it would be restored

3. final
    - the final keyword prevents child classes from overring a method, property or constant by prefixing the definition
    with final. if the class itself is being defined final then it cannot be extended

4. comparing objects
    - when using comparison operator (==): two object instances are equal if they have the same attributes and values,
    and are instances of the same class.
    when using identity operator (===): object variables are identical only if they refer to the same instance of the
    same class.

5. covariance and contravariance
    - covariance allows a child's method return a more specific type than the return type of its parent's method.
    contravariance allows a parameter type to be less specific in a child method than it is in a parent's one.

6. static
    - declaring class properties and methods as static makes them accessible without needing an instantiation of the
    class.

7. lazy objects